## Learning Summary

On the seventh day of the training, our mentor introduced us to several modern concepts that enhance the capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). The session began with **Context Engineering**, where we learned that an AI model produces better responses when it is provided with the right context instead of relying only on the user's prompt. The mentor explained that conversation history, system instructions, retrieved information, and user inputs together help the model generate more accurate and relevant responses.

The mentor then introduced **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)** as a technique that allows AI models to retrieve information from external knowledge sources before generating an answer. Instead of depending entirely on pre-trained knowledge, RAG enables AI systems to access relevant documents or databases, improving the factual accuracy of responses and reducing the chances of incorrect or outdated information.

We were also given a brief overview of **Graph RAG**. Unlike traditional RAG, which retrieves individual pieces of information, Graph RAG organizes knowledge through connected entities and relationships. The mentor explained that this approach helps AI systems understand how different pieces of information are related, making it more suitable for answering complex questions and handling enterprise-level knowledge.

Another important concept discussed during the session was **Prompt Caching**. We learned that frequently used prompts or prompt prefixes can be stored and reused, allowing AI systems to generate responses more quickly while reducing computational cost and improving overall efficiency.

The mentor also explained **Prompt Versioning**, which is used to manage different versions of prompts during AI application development. As prompts are improved over time, versioning helps developers track changes, compare performance, and easily revert to previous versions when required.

Towards the end of the session, we were introduced to **Tool Calling**, where modern AI models can interact with external resources such as search engines, databases, APIs, calculators, and file systems whenever additional information or specific operations are needed. Along with this, the mentor briefly explained the **Semantic Definition of Tools**, emphasizing that AI models require clear descriptions of when and how each tool should be used so that the most appropriate tool is selected for a given task.

Overall, today's session provided an overview of the technologies that make modern AI systems more intelligent, efficient, and capable of solving real-world problems by combining language understanding with external knowledge and specialized tools.

## Key Takeaways

- Context Engineering improves AI responses by providing relevant background information.
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enables AI models to retrieve external knowledge before answering.
- Graph RAG organizes information through relationships between entities for better reasoning.
- Prompt Caching improves efficiency by reusing previously processed prompts.
- Prompt Versioning helps developers manage and improve prompts systematically.
- Tool Calling allows AI models to interact with external tools and services.
- Semantic tool definitions help AI systems choose the correct tool for a given task.

## Outcome

